michelle bachelet's long-awaited report  is a damning indictment of china's  treatment of the uyghur population it  found that uyghurs and other mostly  muslim groups held in detention camps  have been subjected to torture including  forced medical treatment and sexual and  gender-based violence  bachelet's report was commissioned more  than three years ago and was due to be  published last year it was finally  released 30 minutes before her term as  u.n human rights chief ended obviously  there  there's pressure from the chinese  government on the u.n system but it's  the  high commissioner's job to investigate  human rights crimes around the world  including china so we're very glad to  see at least the report report is out  obviously we wish it was  released  sooner  china has condemned the report it says  its policies in xinjiang fight what it  calls terrorism and provide uighurs with  better economic opportunities we all  know  so well that  the so-called xinjiang issue  is completely  is a completely fabricated lie out of  political  motivations and  its purpose definitely  is to undermine china's  stability  and to obstruct china's development  in the past bachelets face criticism for  not being forthright about the  persecution of the uyga community  her visit to china in may was widely  condemned as a whitewash with the u.s  state department calling it a mistake  human rights groups say her report is  better late than never this is a  blueprint really for member states  especially those  who sit on the human rights council to a  doctor resolution that creates an  independent and international mechanism  so that you can start investigating  these crimes and you can start  identifying the perpetrators and  prosecuting those perpetrators  this is the latest of many reports  detailing human rights abuses against  uyghurs  but this one carries the weight of the  un and those campaigning for justice  hope it'll mark a turning point victoria  gatenbee al jazeera
 

